@page "/administration/menu"
@attribute [ExcludeFromInteractiveRouting]
@attribute [Authorize(Policy = AuthorizationPolicies.Administration)]
@using MyRestaurant.DataAccess.Menu
@using MyRestaurant.WebApplication.Menu
@using MyRestaurant.WebApplication.Orders
@inject IMenuSectionDirectory MenuSections
@inject IMenuDirectory MenuItems
@inject IMenuEventLog MenuEvents
@inject IMenuWorkflow MenuChanges
@inject NavigationManager Navigation
@inject RestaurantOptions Options
@inject RestaurantTime RestaurantClock

@* The administration menu index (TECHNICAL_SPECIFICATION §7, §11.4, §11.12 — `area.administration`:
   "Menu (CRUD + activity, event history per item)"). It lists every HEADING with the items filed
   under it, links each of both to its own management page, offers creation of either, and shows the
   recent activity feed that answers "what changed today" without opening an item.

   Static SSR, like every administration surface. Deactivated items are listed, not filtered: §7 is
   explicit that an 86'd item stays on the menu marked "currently unavailable", and a page that hid
   them from the administrator would hide exactly the rows somebody is looking for. The same holds one
   register up, and more strongly: an inactive SECTION is hidden from the guest entirely and from
   §11.4's administrator not at all — whose job on this page may be stocking next week's breakfast
   menu before switching it on.

   **THIS IS SECTIONS-FIRST AS OF M6 SLICE 44, AND THAT IS THE POINT OF THE SURFACE.** Until now it
   was a flat list of every item with the heading as a COLUMN, which is what a menu index looks like
   when the model has just learned to express a menu and the surface has not caught up. Slice 40 shipped
   the column as the honest intermediate and said so: a record list whose rows link nowhere is a list of
   dead ends, so the fact went on the screen and the destination waited for the editor it had to open
   into. The editor landed in Slice 41 and the refile verb in Slice 43. This is the destination.

   **A heading is a `<details>` rendered open, and the item list inside it is §11.12's record list.**
   The disclosure is native: no script on a page that hosts no island, a real disclosure in the
   accessibility tree rather than a div somebody wired up, and no state on the server. It is rendered
   open on every request because a heading collapsed by the server is a heading whose items nobody
   looking for an item can find — and because §16.3 scenario 16 measures what the layout engine laid
   out, so a closed group would quietly remove its own controls from the barrier that exists to find
   exactly that. Collapsing is the operator's decision, not this page's.

   **AN EMPTY HEADING IS VISIBLE HERE FOR THE FIRST TIME.** The list this page used to render was built
   from ITEMS, so a heading with nothing under it appeared nowhere on it — the old code said so in a
   comment beside a count that meant "sections that have something in them". A heading created with a
   typo, or one stocked for next week and not yet filled, was a row this surface could not show. It now
   reads both lists and renders the headings, so emptiness is a state with a sentence rather than an
   absence.

   **THIS PAGE CAN NOW REORDER A HEADING, AND THAT IS WHAT SLICE 47 IS.** For three slices the cut was
   recorded here rather than papered over: `ReorderMenuSectionAsync` sets an ABSOLUTE `display_order`,
   and positions are deliberately non-unique with a name tiebreak (§7), so "move this heading up" is not
   expressible as one absolute write — two headings sharing a position have an order nobody assigned and
   no single number distinguishes them. The verb that expresses it is `ResequenceMenuSectionsAsync`,
   which takes the WHOLE ordering and assigns 0…n-1 from it, and this page is its only caller: the list
   below is already rendered in stored order, so Up and Down exchange two entries in that list and post
   all of it. Nothing is computed from a position, which is the point — a number that means "one less
   than the heading above" cannot be written when two headings share a number.

   Each button is its own form with its own `@@formname`, because Blazor's static SSR dispatches a POST
   to the form that names it and there is one pair per heading. The first heading's Up and the last
   heading's Down are rendered DISABLED rather than omitted: a control that vanishes at the edge of a
   list moves every other control up a row on the render after a move, and §16.3 scenario 16 measures
   where controls are.

   **AND IT CAN NOW REORDER AN ITEM WITHIN ITS HEADING, WHICH IS SLICE 48.** Slice 47 named this as the
   next ordering slice rather than widening itself into it, because `menu_item.display_order` has the
   identical absolute-write shape and the identical non-unique positions but writes to a DIFFERENT event
   table with different paired CHECKs. `ResequenceMenuItemsAsync` takes a heading and the whole ordering
   of the items under it — that heading's list, not the menu's, because a position is a position WITHIN a
   heading and sending the whole menu would renumber the puddings because somebody moved a drink. The
   list each group's loop is already rendering is the list that gets exchanged and posted, so again
   nothing is computed from a position.

   An absolute position is still writable, on the heading's own page and on the item's own page, and that
   is not a duplicate: somebody who wants breakfast at 0 and does not care what else moves is stating an
   absolute, and a number in a form is what that means.

   Two record lists (§11.12) and no inline <style>. The group vocabulary is `.menu-group*` in app.css. *@

<PageTitle>Administration — menu</PageTitle>

<section class="panel panel-wide">
    <div class="page-head">
        <div>
            <p class="eyebrow">Administration</p>
            <h1>Menu</h1>
        </div>
        <AdministrationAreaLinks Current="AdministrationArea.Menu" />
    </div>

    <div class="page-head-action">
        <a class="button-primary" href="/administration/menu/new">Create menu item</a>
        <a class="button-secondary" href="/administration/menu/sections/new">Create section</a>
    </div>

    @if (_flashMessage is not null)
    {
        <p class="status-success" role="status">@_flashMessage</p>
    }
    @if (_errorMessage is not null)
    {
        <p class="status-error" role="alert">@_errorMessage</p>
    }

    <p class="lede">
        Every heading, in the order guests read them, with the items filed under each in the order they
        appear beneath it. Move a heading with the buttons at the foot of its group, and an item with the
        Up and Down on its own row — either way the whole order is rewritten from the list you can see, so
        a move needs no positions typed anywhere. An item only ever moves within its own heading; refiling
        it under a different one is on the item's page. Prices here are what the <em>next</em> order line
        costs — anything already on a bill keeps the price it was added at. Open a heading to rename it,
        describe it, set an absolute position or switch it off; open an item to rename it, reprice it,
        describe it, refile it, set an absolute position, or mark it unavailable.
    </p>

    @if (_sections is null)
    {
        <p class="record-empty">Loading…</p>
    }
    else if (_sections.Count == 0)
    {
        <p class="record-empty">
            No headings yet, so there is nowhere for an item to go. Every menu item sits under a
            heading — drinks, entrees, breakfast — so name the first one and the menu starts there.
        </p>
        <div class="form-actions">
            <a class="button-primary" href="/administration/menu/sections/new">Create the first section</a>
        </div>
    }
    else
    {
        <div class="menu-groups">
            @foreach (MenuSectionSummary menuSectionSummary in _sections)
            {
                @* @key on the section identifier rather than the loop index: a heading that moves
                   position between renders would otherwise be reconciled as an edit of whichever
                   heading now sits where it used to. *@
                <details class="menu-group" open @key="menuSectionSummary.MenuSectionIdentifier">
                    <summary class="menu-group-summary">
                        <span class="menu-group-name">@menuSectionSummary.Name</span>
                        <span class="menu-group-meta">
                            @if (menuSectionSummary.IsActive)
                            {
                                <span class="chip chip-ok">Visible to guests</span>
                            }
                            else
                            {
                                @* §7's asymmetry, worded as the consequence rather than as the flag —
                                   the same wording the editor uses. "Inactive" is what the column says;
                                   "hidden from guests" is what it does, and it is the opposite of what
                                   the same word does one table away, where an inactive ITEM stays on the
                                   guest's menu and is marked. *@
                                <span class="chip chip-warn">Hidden from guests</span>
                            }
                            <span class="menu-group-count">
                                @ItemCountLabel(menuSectionSummary) · position @menuSectionSummary.DisplayOrder
                            </span>
                        </span>
                    </summary>

                    <div class="menu-group-body">
                        @if (menuSectionSummary.Description.Length > 0)
                        {
                            <p class="menu-group-description">@menuSectionSummary.Description</p>
                        }

                        @{
                            IReadOnlyList<MenuItemSummary> items = ItemsUnder(menuSectionSummary);
                        }

                        @if (items.Count == 0)
                        {
                            <p class="record-empty">
                                Nothing is filed under this heading yet. §11.1 renders no empty heading
                                to a guest, so this one reaches nobody whatever its visibility says
                                above — which is why it is worth seeing here.
                            </p>
                        }
                        else
                        {
                            <div class="record-list">
                                <table>
                                    <thead>
                                        <tr>
                                            <th scope="col">Item</th>
                                            <th scope="col">Price</th>
                                            <th scope="col">Availability</th>
                                            <th scope="col">Position</th>
                                            <th scope="col"><span class="visually-hidden">Actions</span></th>
                                        </tr>
                                    </thead>
                                    <tbody>
                                        @foreach (MenuItemSummary item in items)
                                        {
                                            <tr @key="item.MenuItemIdentifier">
                                                <td class="record-primary" data-label="">
                                                    <a class="record-link" href="@ManagePath(item.MenuItemIdentifier)">@item.Name</a>
                                                    @if (item.Description.Length > 0)
                                                    {
                                                        @* Under the name rather than in a column of its
                                                           own. A description is a sentence, and a
                                                           sentence in a table cell at 375px is the shape
                                                           F-59 was about; this way the handheld card
                                                           reads name-then-sentence and the wide table
                                                           reads it as a subtitle. *@
                                                        <span class="record-secondary">@item.Description</span>
                                                    }
                                                </td>
                                                <td class="record-numeric" data-label="Price">@Money(item.PriceAmount)</td>
                                                <td data-label="">
                                                    @if (item.IsActive)
                                                    {
                                                        <span class="chip chip-ok">Available</span>
                                                    }
                                                    else
                                                    {
                                                        <span class="chip chip-warn">Currently unavailable</span>
                                                    }
                                                </td>
                                                <td class="record-numeric" data-label="Position">@item.DisplayOrder</td>
                                                <td class="record-actions" data-label="">
                                                    @* Two posts and a link, the same three controls a
                                                       heading's group carries, one register down. The
                                                       forms are named from the ITEM's identifier, so a
                                                       menu with sixty dishes has a hundred and twenty
                                                       distinct form names and Blazor's static SSR routes
                                                       each POST to the row that owns it. The ends are
                                                       disabled rather than omitted, on the rule the
                                                       group's row already follows. *@
                                                    <form method="post" @formname="@ItemMoveFormName(item, up: true)"
                                                          @onsubmit="@(() => MoveItemAsync(menuSectionSummary.MenuSectionIdentifier, item.MenuItemIdentifier, -1))">
                                                        <AntiforgeryToken />
                                                        <button type="submit" class="button-secondary"
                                                                disabled="@IsFirstUnder(menuSectionSummary, item)"
                                                                aria-label="@($"Move {item.Name} up")">Up</button>
                                                    </form>
                                                    <form method="post" @formname="@ItemMoveFormName(item, up: false)"
                                                          @onsubmit="@(() => MoveItemAsync(menuSectionSummary.MenuSectionIdentifier, item.MenuItemIdentifier, 1))">
                                                        <AntiforgeryToken />
                                                        <button type="submit" class="button-secondary"
                                                                disabled="@IsLastUnder(menuSectionSummary, item)"
                                                                aria-label="@($"Move {item.Name} down")">Down</button>
                                                    </form>
                                                    <a class="button-secondary" href="@ManagePath(item.MenuItemIdentifier)"
                                                       aria-label="@($"Manage {item.Name}")">Manage</a>
                                                </td>
                                            </tr>
                                        }
                                    </tbody>
                                </table>
                            </div>
                        }

                        <div class="menu-group-actions">
                            @* Two posts and a link. The forms carry `@@formname` values built from the
                               heading's identifier because there is one pair per group and Blazor's
                               static SSR routes a POST by that name; the handler receives the identifier
                               rather than a position, and computes the exchange against the very list
                               this loop is rendering. *@
                            <form method="post" @formname="@MoveFormName(menuSectionSummary, up: true)"
                                  @onsubmit="@(() => MoveAsync(menuSectionSummary.MenuSectionIdentifier, -1))">
                                <AntiforgeryToken />
                                <button type="submit" class="button-secondary" disabled="@IsFirst(menuSectionSummary)"
                                        aria-label="@($"Move {menuSectionSummary.Name} up")">Move up</button>
                            </form>
                            <form method="post" @formname="@MoveFormName(menuSectionSummary, up: false)"
                                  @onsubmit="@(() => MoveAsync(menuSectionSummary.MenuSectionIdentifier, 1))">
                                <AntiforgeryToken />
                                <button type="submit" class="button-secondary" disabled="@IsLast(menuSectionSummary)"
                                        aria-label="@($"Move {menuSectionSummary.Name} down")">Move down</button>
                            </form>
                            <a class="button-secondary" href="@SectionPath(menuSectionSummary.MenuSectionIdentifier)"
                               aria-label="@($"Manage {menuSectionSummary.Name}")">Manage this heading</a>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </details>
            }
        </div>

        <p class="record-note">
            @_itemCount @(_itemCount == 1 ? "item" : "items") across @_sections.Count
            @(_sections.Count == 1 ? "heading" : "headings"), @_activeItemCount available and
            @_describedItemCount described. An unavailable item stays on the guest's menu marked
            unavailable and cannot be added to a send — it is never hidden and never deleted, so past
            orders keep the item they were placed against. A heading switched off takes everything under
            it off the guest's menu and changes none of those items.
        </p>
    }

    <hr class="record-rule" />

    <h2 class="record-heading">Recent activity</h2>
    <p class="lede">
        The last @RecentActivityCount changes across the whole menu, newest first. Each item's complete
        history is on its own page, and each heading's is on its own.
    </p>

    @if (_recent is null)
    {
        <p class="record-empty">Loading…</p>
    }
    else if (_recent.Count == 0)
    {
        <p class="record-empty">Nothing has changed yet.</p>
    }
    else
    {
        <div class="record-list">
            <table>
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="col">Item</th>
                        <th scope="col">Change</th>
                        <th scope="col">When</th>
                        <th scope="col">By</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    @foreach (MenuItemEventEntry entry in _recent)
                    {
                        @* @key is on the event identifier rather than the loop index: two events on the
                           same item in the same second are ordered by their UUIDv7 keys (§8.2), and an
                           index would make the second one look like an edit of the first. *@
                        <tr @key="entry.MenuItemEventIdentifier">
                            <td class="record-primary" data-label="">
                                <a class="record-link" href="@ManagePath(entry.MenuItemIdentifier)">@entry.MenuItemName</a>
                            </td>
                            <td data-label="Change">@Describe(entry)</td>
                            <td data-label="When">@RestaurantClock.DateAndTime(entry.OccurredAt)</td>
                            <td data-label="By">@entry.ActorName</td>
                        </tr>
                    }
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>

        <p class="record-note">
            This feed is the ITEM log only. A heading's own changes — renamed, described, moved, shown,
            hidden — are on that heading's page, because `menu_section_event` has no cross-section feed:
            no surface wanted one, and a read with no caller is the same defect as a workflow verb with
            no caller (§7).
        </p>
    }

    <p><a href="/administration">Back to administration</a></p>
</section>

@code {
    /// <summary>
    /// Enough to cover a service's worth of 86s without turning the index into the archive. §11.4's
    /// "filters narrow only on explicit request" governs the per-item history, which is uncapped; this
    /// panel is a glance, and its cap is the explicit request.
    /// </summary>
    private const int RecentActivityCount = 20;

    private IReadOnlyList<MenuSectionSummary>? _sections;
    private IReadOnlyList<MenuItemSummary> _items = [];
    private IReadOnlyList<MenuItemEventEntry>? _recent;
    private int _itemCount;
    private int _activeItemCount;
    private int _describedItemCount;
    private string? _flashMessage;
    private string? _errorMessage;

    [CascadingParameter]
    private HttpContext HttpContext { get; set; } = default!;

    [SupplyParameterFromQuery]
    private string? Done { get; set; }

    protected override async Task OnInitializedAsync()
    {
        // Two reads rather than one, and the second is the one this surface gained. IMenuDirectory
        // builds its list from menu_item, so a heading with nothing under it is not in it at all —
        // which is precisely the row an administrator wondering where their new heading went is looking
        // for. IMenuSectionDirectory is the list of headings themselves, empty ones included, and its
        // own doc comment says that is what it is for.
        _sections = await MenuSections.ListAsync(HttpContext.RequestAborted);
        _items = await MenuItems.ListAsync(HttpContext.RequestAborted);

        _itemCount = _items.Count;
        _activeItemCount = _items.Count(item => item.IsActive);
        _describedItemCount = _items.Count(item => item.Description.Length > 0);

        _recent = await MenuEvents.ListRecentAsync(RecentActivityCount, HttpContext.RequestAborted);

        // Post/redirect/get, the shape every administration write on this tree uses: a committed move
        // redirects back here with one word, so a refresh cannot re-post it. On a POST the handler below
        // is about to run and there is nothing to say yet.
        if (HttpMethods.IsGet(HttpContext.Request.Method))
        {
            _flashMessage = FlashFor(Done);
        }
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Exchanges one heading with its neighbour and posts the <b>whole</b> ordering (§7).
    ///
    /// <para><b>The list is the source of the move, which is why this takes an offset rather than a
    /// position.</b> <c>_sections</c> is what the page rendered, in the order §7's read produced; the
    /// exchange happens in a copy of it and the result is sent as the complete ordering. So the write is
    /// expressible even when two headings share a <c>display_order</c> — which the schema permits on
    /// purpose, and which is the whole reason an absolute write could not express it.</para>
    ///
    /// <para><b>Every failure here is a stale page rather than a fault, and each reloads.</b> An identifier
    /// this render does not hold, or a neighbour past either end, means somebody else changed the menu
    /// between this page being drawn and the button being pressed. The same is true of
    /// <c>MenuSectionSetChanged</c>, which the write service returns when the ordering is no longer a
    /// permutation of the stored set — it refuses rather than reconciling, and the honest answer on this
    /// side is to render the truth again.</para>
    /// </summary>
    private async Task MoveAsync(Guid menuSectionIdentifier, int offset)
    {
        if (_sections is null)
        {
            Navigation.NavigateTo(IndexPath);
            return;
        }

        if (PersonPrincipal.IdentifierFor(HttpContext.User) is not { } administrator)
        {
            // Endpoint authorization already guarantees this; menu_section_event.actor_person_identifier
            // is NOT NULL and §7 requires every change to name somebody, so this is the belt to that brace
            // — the same helper ManageMenuSection carries, in the one form this page needs.
            _errorMessage = "Your session could not be identified. Sign in again and retry.";
            return;
        }

        List<Guid> ordering = _sections.Select(summary => summary.MenuSectionIdentifier).ToList();

        int from = ordering.IndexOf(menuSectionIdentifier);
        int to = from + offset;

        if (from < 0 || to < 0 || to >= ordering.Count)
        {
            Navigation.NavigateTo(IndexPath);
            return;
        }

        (ordering[from], ordering[to]) = (ordering[to], ordering[from]);

        ResequenceMenuSectionsOutcome outcome = await MenuChanges.ResequenceMenuSectionsAsync(
            ordering, administrator, HttpContext.RequestAborted);

        switch (outcome)
        {
            case ResequenceMenuSectionsOutcome.Resequenced:
                RedirectWithOutcome("moved");
                break;
            case ResequenceMenuSectionsOutcome.NoChange:
                RedirectWithOutcome("move-no-change");
                break;
            case ResequenceMenuSectionsOutcome.MenuSectionSetChanged:
                RedirectWithOutcome("menu-changed");
                break;
        }
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Exchanges one item with its neighbour <b>under the same heading</b> and posts that heading's whole
    /// ordering (§7).
    ///
    /// <para><b>The same shape as <see cref="MoveAsync"/> one register down, and the one difference is the
    /// set.</b> A position is a position <em>within</em> a heading, so the list exchanged is
    /// <see cref="ItemsUnder"/>'s — not <c>_items</c>, which is the whole menu and whose neighbours cross
    /// heading boundaries. Sending the whole menu would ask the write service to renumber the puddings
    /// because somebody moved a drink.</para>
    ///
    /// <para><b>Every failure here is a stale page rather than a fault, and each reloads.</b> An identifier
    /// this render does not hold under this heading, or a neighbour past either end, means somebody else
    /// changed the menu between this page being drawn and the button being pressed — as does
    /// <c>MenuItemSetChanged</c>, which the write service returns when the ordering is no longer a
    /// permutation of what that heading holds. The honest answer on this side is to render the truth
    /// again.</para>
    /// </summary>
    private async Task MoveItemAsync(Guid menuSectionIdentifier, Guid menuItemIdentifier, int offset)
    {
        if (_sections is null)
        {
            Navigation.NavigateTo(IndexPath);
            return;
        }

        if (PersonPrincipal.IdentifierFor(HttpContext.User) is not { } administrator)
        {
            // As MoveAsync: endpoint authorization already guarantees this, and
            // menu_item_event.actor_person_identifier is NOT NULL.
            _errorMessage = "Your session could not be identified. Sign in again and retry.";
            return;
        }

        List<Guid> ordering = _items
            .Where(item => item.MenuSectionIdentifier == menuSectionIdentifier)
            .Select(item => item.MenuItemIdentifier)
            .ToList();

        int from = ordering.IndexOf(menuItemIdentifier);
        int to = from + offset;

        if (from < 0 || to < 0 || to >= ordering.Count)
        {
            Navigation.NavigateTo(IndexPath);
            return;
        }

        (ordering[from], ordering[to]) = (ordering[to], ordering[from]);

        ResequenceMenuItemsOutcome outcome = await MenuChanges.ResequenceMenuItemsAsync(
            menuSectionIdentifier, ordering, administrator, HttpContext.RequestAborted);

        switch (outcome)
        {
            case ResequenceMenuItemsOutcome.Resequenced:
                RedirectWithOutcome("item-moved");
                break;
            case ResequenceMenuItemsOutcome.NoChange:
                RedirectWithOutcome("item-move-no-change");
                break;
            case ResequenceMenuItemsOutcome.MenuItemSetChanged:
                RedirectWithOutcome("items-changed");
                break;
        }
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// The items filed under one heading, in the order guests read them.
    ///
    /// <para><b>Filtered rather than grouped, and rather than asked for per section.</b> §7's read
    /// orders by <c>(section.display_order, section.name, section.menu_section_identifier,
    /// item.display_order, item.name, item.menu_item_identifier)</c>, so this filter preserves the order
    /// somebody chose without re-deciding it — where a <c>GroupBy</c> would re-order the headings into
    /// hash order and make the ordering decision a second time in a second file. A per-section read
    /// would be a query with one caller, which is the defect <see cref="IMenuDirectory"/> declines to
    /// invent for exactly this reason. The menu is single-digit-to-sixty rows on the machine this runs
    /// on, and the same filter is what <c>ManageMenuSection</c> already does.</para>
    /// </summary>
    private IReadOnlyList<MenuItemSummary> ItemsUnder(MenuSectionSummary section)
        => _items.Where(item => item.MenuSectionIdentifier == section.MenuSectionIdentifier).ToArray();

    /// <summary>
    /// How many items are under a heading, as a phrase rather than a bare number, because it sits in a
    /// summary line beside a position and two adjacent integers with no words between them are
    /// unreadable.
    /// </summary>
    private string ItemCountLabel(MenuSectionSummary section)
    {
        int count = ItemsUnder(section).Count;

        return count switch
        {
            0 => "no items",
            1 => "1 item",
            _ => $"{count} items",
        };
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Whether a heading is at an end of the list, which is what disables one of its two buttons. Compared
    /// by identifier rather than by <c>display_order</c>, and that is the same distinction the whole verb
    /// rests on: two headings may share a position, so "has the lowest number" is not "is first" — but
    /// "is the first entry of the list this page is rendering" is exactly what Up would exchange with
    /// nothing.
    /// </summary>
    private bool IsFirst(MenuSectionSummary menuSectionSummary)
        => _sections is null
            || _sections.Count == 0
            || _sections[0].MenuSectionIdentifier == menuSectionSummary.MenuSectionIdentifier;

    private bool IsLast(MenuSectionSummary menuSectionSummary)
        => _sections is null
            || _sections.Count == 0
            || _sections[^1].MenuSectionIdentifier == menuSectionSummary.MenuSectionIdentifier;

    /// <summary>
    /// One form name per heading per direction. Unique within the page because the identifier is, which is
    /// what Blazor's static SSR needs to route a POST to the right handler; a shared name would dispatch
    /// every group's button to whichever form rendered first.
    /// </summary>
    private static string MoveFormName(MenuSectionSummary menuSectionSummary, bool up)
        => $"menu-section-move-{(up ? "up" : "down")}-{menuSectionSummary.MenuSectionIdentifier}";

    /// <summary>
    /// Whether an item is at an end of <b>its own heading's</b> list, which is what disables one of its two
    /// buttons. Compared by identifier against the filtered list for exactly the reason
    /// <see cref="IsFirst"/> gives: two items may share a <c>display_order</c>, so "has the lowest number"
    /// is not "is first", while "is the first entry of the list this group is rendering" is precisely what
    /// Up would exchange with nothing.
    /// </summary>
    private bool IsFirstUnder(MenuSectionSummary section, MenuItemSummary item)
    {
        IReadOnlyList<MenuItemSummary> items = ItemsUnder(section);

        return items.Count == 0 || items[0].MenuItemIdentifier == item.MenuItemIdentifier;
    }

    private bool IsLastUnder(MenuSectionSummary section, MenuItemSummary item)
    {
        IReadOnlyList<MenuItemSummary> items = ItemsUnder(section);

        return items.Count == 0 || items[^1].MenuItemIdentifier == item.MenuItemIdentifier;
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// One form name per item per direction, built from the item's identifier rather than from its heading's
    /// — the item identifier is unique across the whole page, so no two rows can collide even when the same
    /// dish name appears under two headings.
    /// </summary>
    private static string ItemMoveFormName(MenuItemSummary item, bool up)
        => $"menu-item-move-{(up ? "up" : "down")}-{item.MenuItemIdentifier}";

    private static string IndexPath => "/administration/menu";

    private void RedirectWithOutcome(string outcome)
        => Navigation.NavigateTo($"{IndexPath}?done={outcome}");

    /// <summary>
    /// The word the redirect carried, as a sentence. Three outcomes and three sentences, because the two
    /// that wrote nothing are ordinary events on a shared menu rather than errors: somebody else moved the
    /// heading first, or somebody else changed which headings there are.
    /// </summary>
    private static string? FlashFor(string? outcome) => outcome switch
    {
        "moved" => "Moved. Every open menu has already picked up the new order.",
        "move-no-change" => "It was already in that position — nothing changed.",
        "menu-changed" => "The headings changed while this page was open, so nothing was moved. This is"
            + " the current order; try again.",
        "item-moved" => "Moved. Every open menu has already picked up the new order.",
        "item-move-no-change" => "It was already in that position — nothing changed.",
        "items-changed" => "The items under that heading changed while this page was open, so nothing was"
            + " moved. This is the current order; try again.",
        _ => null,
    };

    private static string ManagePath(Guid menuItemIdentifier)
        => $"/administration/menu/{menuItemIdentifier}";

    private static string SectionPath(Guid menuSectionIdentifier)
        => $"/administration/menu/sections/{menuSectionIdentifier}";

    private string Money(decimal amount) => MoneyText.Format(amount, Options.CurrencyCode);

    /// <summary>
    /// A friendly label for each event type §8.2's <c>menu_item_event</c> vocabulary CHECK admits,
    /// falling back to the stored string for anything else — §11.4 renders the record, so an
    /// unrecognised type must show as itself rather than be swallowed. The arms below are deliberately
    /// not counted in prose: this comment said <em>five</em> and named <em>two</em> new types where the
    /// plan specified three, in a sentence nothing in the tree could check (F-77).
    ///
    /// <para>This feed is a glance rather than an audit, so a <c>description_changed</c> says that a
    /// description moved without quoting it — the sentence belongs on the item's own page, where the
    /// uncapped history renders it in full.</para>
    /// </summary>
    private string Describe(MenuItemEventEntry entry) => entry.EventType switch
    {
        "created" => $"Created at {Money(entry.NewPriceAmount ?? 0m)}",
        "name_changed" => $"Renamed to {entry.NewName}",
        "price_changed" => $"Repriced to {Money(entry.NewPriceAmount ?? 0m)}",
        "description_changed" => (entry.NewDescription ?? string.Empty).Length == 0
            ? "Description cleared"
            : "Description changed",
        "section_changed" => $"Filed under {entry.NewMenuSectionName}",
        "reordered" => $"Moved to position {entry.NewDisplayOrder}",
        "activated" => "Marked available",
        "deactivated" => "Marked unavailable",
        _ => entry.EventType,
    };
}
